<!--
  Direct peering between constellations, no IXP satellite.

  AS102-105 form a full mesh with a main and a standby cable per pair: 6 pairs x 2 cables = 12
  mesh links, plus 4 backbone links to the ground ASes. Six adjacencies in all.

  IF ID convention: units digit is the role (0 = main, 1 = standby); the hundreds digit of the
  last three digits is the location, which is always 1 here.

  SINGLE-LOCATION, deliberately. The mesh was built at two locations, giving each AS pair two
  concurrent adjacencies. libbgp scopes its RIB by peer router ID and router IDs are per node,
  so two concurrent sessions between one AS pair share a scope and overwrite each other:
  peering both blackholed AS102-AS105 and AS103-AS105 at 97-100% loss with no churn, and
  peering one left every location-B handover invisible to BGP. See BuildTopologyLinksDirect().

  The control case for the IXP comparison: an exchange satellite gives full connectivity with
  one adjacency per constellation, whereas direct peering needs one per pair. That
  O(n^2)-vs-O(n) difference is a reported result, not a confound to normalise away.

  GENERATED by utils/bgp_topology_to_scion_xml.py from the link table written
  by the BGP scenario program's dumpTopology flag. Do not edit by hand:
  regenerate it, so the BGP and SCION graphs cannot drift apart.
-->
